    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment",
    "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-t}+6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =-4{e}^{-2}+6\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2}+10 \\\\  & =9.458\\,658… \\\\  & ≈9.46\\end{aligned}\n$$",
